ly  the one piece is again the irreducible  error that comes from the random  variation in the in the new test point y  zero about the true function f  but these other two pieces  break up the reducible part of the error  what we call the reducible part before  into two components  one is called the variance of f-hat  and that's the variance that comes from  having different training sets if i got  a new training set and i fit my model  again i'd have a different function f  hat  and so as i  would if i were to look at many many  different training sets there would be  variability in my prediction at x zero  and then a quantity called the bias of  of f hat and what the bias is  is the difference between the average  prediction at x naught averaged over all  these different training sets and the  true f at x naught  and what you have is typically as the  flexibility of f at increases its  variance increases because it's going  after the individual training set that  you've provided  which will of course be different from  the next training set  but its bias decreases  so choosing the flexibility based on  average test error amounts to what we  call a bias variance trade-off  this will come up  a lot in  future parts of the course  for those three examples  we see the bias variance trade-off  again in this plot the red curve is the  mean squared error in the test data  and then below it we have the two  components of that mean squared error  the two important components which are  the bias and the variance  and  in the in the left plot we've got the  the  the bias decreasing  and then flattening off  as we get more flexible and the variance  increase in and when you add those two  components you get the u-shaped curve  and  in the in the middle and and last plots  that correspond to the the the other two  examples  um  the same decomposition is given and  because the nature of that problem  changed  the the trade-off is changing  okay so we've seen now that choosing the  amount of flexibility of a model  amounts to a bias variance trade-off  and depending on the problem we might  want to make that trade-off in in a  different place  and we we're gonna we can use  a validation set or or  left out data to help us make that  choice but that's a choice that's the  choice that needs to be made to select  the model  now we've been  addressing this in terms of regression  problems in the next segment we're going  to see how all this works for  classification problems
 

